<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William I of Sicily</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1131–1166</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as William the Bad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, called the Bad, was King of Sicily from 1154 to 1166, succeeding his father Roger II. His reign faced baronial revolts and a Byzantine invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, which he defeated in 1156. During his reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> took Roger II's North African possessions, ending with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a> in January 1160.</p></div>
